In this lesson we'll look at how to actually solve quadratic equations, and there are two main ways to think about it. First, there's the graphical way. If you sketch the parabola that comes from your quadratic, the solutions are simply where that curve crosses the horizontal axis — those crossing points are called the x-intercepts. So you can plot it, eyeball where it hits zero, and if you're not sure exactly, just try a few values on either side and narrow it down. Nice and visual. Second, there's the algebraic way, and it leans on a simple idea called the null factor law. That just says if two things multiplied together equal zero, then one of them has to be zero. So if you can rewrite your quadratic as two brackets multiplied together, you set each bracket to zero and solve. For a monic quadratic — meaning the coefficient out the front is one — you want two numbers that multiply to the constant term and add up to the coefficient of x. Once you spot those numbers, the factorising is easy, and the answers fall out. One quick tip on signs: if that constant is positive, your two numbers share the same sign, and if it's negative, they have opposite signs. And if the parabola only just touches the axis, you get one repeated solution. Now let's see it in action.